;
; Demo Reel 3's own ToRAM, one line at a time, with somewhere to speak.
;
; The icon runs C:IconX on the `DemoReel` script, which is three lines:
;
;     execute ToRAM
;     C:run >NIL:  DemoReelData:Slish
;     EndCLI >NIL:
;
; A window opens and nothing is written, which is what both a script that
; died on its first line and a script whose output went nowhere look like.
; IconX passes SYS_Output as NULL, so there is a real chance nothing the
; script says ever reaches that window at all.
;
; ToRAM's first act is the 1989 idiom for "assign this to here":
;
;     if exists Monument
;     c:assign DemoReel3: ""
;     c:assign DemoReelData: ""
;     endif
;
; An empty target. On AmigaOS 1.3 that is the current directory; on AROS,
; `Assign NAME:` with no target REMOVES the assign, so if ReadArgs drops the
; empty string rather than keeping it, the two assigns the whole demo is
; built on are never made -- and every line after it fails against a device
; that does not exist.
;
; This runs the same steps against DEBUG: and reports each one, including the
; explicit-path form of the assign, so one boot says which half is broken.
;
CD SYS:DemoReel3
Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] --- the drawer"
If EXISTS "Monument"
    Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] Monument is here, so ToRAM would do the assigns"
Else
    Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] NO Monument -- wrong directory, nothing else matters"
EndIf

Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] --- assign with an empty target, as the demo writes it"
Assign >DEBUG: DemoReel3: ""
Assign >DEBUG: DemoReelData: ""
If EXISTS "DemoReel3:Monument"
    Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] the empty-target assign WORKED"
Else
    Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] the empty-target assign did NOT take"
    Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] --- same assign, with the path spelled out"
    Assign >DEBUG: DemoReel3: SYS:DemoReel3
    Assign >DEBUG: DemoReelData: SYS:DemoReel3
    If EXISTS "DemoReel3:Monument"
        Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] the explicit form WORKED -- it is the empty target"
    Else
        Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] the explicit form failed too -- it is not the assign"
    EndIf
EndIf

Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] --- the assigns as the system sees them"
Assign >DEBUG:

Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] --- DoWeHaveMem (sets WARN below 1 MB)"
DemoReel3:DoWeHaveMem
If WARN
    Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] DoWeHaveMem said WARN: not enough memory"
Else
    Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] DoWeHaveMem was happy"
EndIf

Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] --- the twelve files ToRAM puts in RAM:"
;
; All twelve, not one. The first version of this script copied dr3 alone and
; then ran Slish, which returned at once -- and that told us nothing, because
; Slish was looking for eleven files that were not there. A probe that skips
; the setup and then reports on the thing that needed it is worse than no
; probe: it produces an answer.
;
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReel3:centisec TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReel3:mt1 TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReel3:mt2 TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReel3:mt3 TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReel3:mt4 TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReel3:mt5 TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReel3:mt6 TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReel3:mt7 TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReel3:crash TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReelData:slide TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReelData:wall.cu TO RAM:
Copy >DEBUG: DemoReelData:dr3 TO RAM:
Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] --- what RAM: holds now"
List >DEBUG: RAM: LFORMAT "%N %L"

;
; The demo runs this as `C:run >NIL: DemoReelData:Slish` -- asynchronous and
; with its output thrown away, so a crash here is silent by construction.
; Run it in the foreground, talking, with the setup it actually expects.
;

;
; What Slish opens, before asking it to run.
;
; Its own strings name five: intuition.library ("1.2 or higher"),
; dos.library, graphics.library, timer.device and audio.device. Four of
; those are here. audio.device is not -- not on the card, not in the kernel
; ELF -- because this target builds neither implementation: AROS's AHI-backed
; one is excluded for m68k on purpose ("do not override ROM Paula
; audio.device") and the real Paula one lives under arch/m68k-amiga, which is
; a different target.
;
; That is a fact. Whether it is why Slish quits is not: every failure Slish
; has a string for prints something, and it printed nothing at all. So ask
; each one by name and let the log say which is missing, rather than
; assuming the interesting one is the answer.
;
Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] --- what Slish opens"
Version >DEBUG: intuition.library
Version >DEBUG: dos.library
Version >DEBUG: graphics.library
Version >DEBUG: timer.device
Version >DEBUG: audio.device

Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] --- Slish, in the foreground"
DemoReelData:Slish >DEBUG:
If ERROR
    Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] Slish returned ERROR"
Else
    If WARN
        Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] Slish returned WARN"
    Else
        Echo >DEBUG: "[dr3] Slish returned OK -- it thinks it succeeded"
    EndIf
EndIf
